e-patch-management walkthrough log — 2026-10-10
Worked-case lesson: no command line by design (brief §6).
The "run" is the patching SLA policy + exception register + emergency runbook
authored and self-reviewed in-session against NIST SP 800-40 Rev. 4's program
structure and the CISA KEV catalog's prioritization model.

FACTUAL CORRECTION vs research spec (verified in-session 2026-10-10):
  The spec's frame predates a real change: CISA's BOD 22-01 page now states
  "This Directive has been revoked. It is superseded by BOD 26-04" (revoked
  June 10, 2026). BOD 26-04 "Prioritizing Security Updates Based on Risk"
  (fetched: https://www.cisa.gov/news-events/directives/bod-26-04-prioritizing-security-updates-based-risk,
  84350 chars) "supersedes and hereby revokes BOD 19-02 ... and BOD 22-01 ...
  consolidates and clarifies vulnerability remediation guidelines for federal
  agencies." The lesson teaches KEV + BOD 26-04 and notes the revocation.

Scope: fictional Northgate Retail (300 staff, 40 stores, e-commerce, POS).
Artifacts authored (all fictional, labeled):
  - SLA policy: 4 tiers (Emergency 24h / Critical 7d / High 30d / Routine 90d)
    with triggers and scope notes; timelines explicitly the company's own
    decisions, not CISA's.
  - Testing/windows: POS overnight vendor window; e-commerce canary→10%→full
    with auto-rollback; warehouse monthly window.
  - Exception register: 2 rows (2008 warehouse server — isolated VLAN, no
    internet, host IDS, quarterly review, named owner; POS firmware awaiting
    vendor — 90-day with ticket ref).
  - Emergency runbook: declaration authority, non-waivable minimum testing
    (canary + smoke), communications, rollback thresholds; annual tabletop
    (last exercise found the stale store-notification list — fixed).

